<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Brain Injury Guides</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by our editorial team. Updated May 2026.</em></p>

  <p>Four substantive guides cover the medical and legal landscape of traumatic brain injury cases. Each is written for non-lawyer, non-clinician readers but cites primary sources (Glasgow Coma Scale, Rancho Los Amigos Scale, Disability Rating Scale, AANS/CNS guidelines, life-care planning textbooks, state catastrophic injury statutes, and the published verdict and settlement databases) that an attorney or paralegal can verify directly.</p>

  <h2>How brain injury settlements are calculated</h2>
  <p>The detailed methodology guide. Walks through the five damages components (past medicals, future medicals, lost earnings, pain and suffering, loss of consortium), the life-care planning process, jurisdictional adjustments, and how the calculator on the home page arrives at its range. Includes worked examples for moderate and severe TBI cases.</p>
  <p><a href="/guides/how-brain-injury-settlements-are-calculated/">Read the settlement calculation guide &rarr;</a></p>

  <h2>Types of traumatic brain injuries and prognosis</h2>
  <p>Medical classification reference. Mild, moderate, and severe TBI by Glasgow Coma Scale. Focal vs diffuse injury. Primary vs secondary injury. Major injury patterns: concussion, diffuse axonal injury, contusion, subdural hematoma, epidural hematoma, anoxic injury, penetrating injury. Recovery curves, prognosis indicators, and the specific challenges of mild TBI with negative imaging.</p>
  <p><a href="/guides/types-of-traumatic-brain-injuries-and-prognosis/">Read the injury types and prognosis guide &rarr;</a></p>

  <h2>What to do after a brain injury</h2>
  <p>Practical guidance for the patient and family in the days, weeks, and months after a brain injury. Medical priorities (acute care, neuro-rehabilitation, neuropsychological evaluation). Evidence preservation (imaging, witness statements, scene documentation). Attorney consultation. Insurance coordination. SSDI and disability benefits. Long-term care planning, including special needs trust considerations. Family roles and the practical realities of caregiving.</p>
  <p><a href="/guides/what-to-do-after-brain-injury/">Read the post-injury practical guide &rarr;</a></p>

  <h2>Common brain injury claim misconceptions</h2>
  <p>Fifteen common myths and misconceptions about brain injury claims, addressed directly. Why mild TBI is not "minor." Why a negative CT does not rule out brain injury. The legitimacy of post-concussive syndrome. The role of insurance limits. Comparative fault. Pediatric vs adult cases. Second impact syndrome. Settlement timing. Attorney fee structures. Tax treatment of recoveries.</p>
  <p><a href="/guides/common-brain-injury-claim-misconceptions/">Read the misconceptions guide &rarr;</a></p>

